Install
pip install oracledb
Then try Thin mode first
import oracledb

conn = oracledb.connect(
    user="CES",
    password="ces",
    dsn="ces_tst"
)

cur = conn.cursor()
cur.execute("select sysdate from dual")
print(cur.fetchone())
conn.close()

If ces_tst is a TNS alias and your Oracle networking is configured correctly, this may work immediately.

If not, we'll switch to Thick mode:

import oracledb

oracledb.init_oracle_client(
    lib_dir=r"C:\oracle\product\12.1.0\client_1\bin"
)

conn = oracledb.connect(
    user="CES",
    password="ces",
    dsn="ces_tst"
)

Because your Python is 32-bit and your Oracle 12.1 client is 32-bit, this combination is fully supported.

For SQL Server, I'd like one more test

Instead of connecting to the server, check whether the driver can even be loaded:

import pyodbc

driver = "ODBC Driver 17 for SQL Server"

try:
    conn = pyodbc.connect(
        f"DRIVER={{{driver}}};",
        timeout=3
    )
except Exception as ex:
    print(repr(ex))

Also run this command from a Command Prompt:

odbcconf /Lv

and tell me whether it runs or reports an error.
